Falsifies `pci::verification::ecam_offset_stays_inside_the_window`.

The defect: the bus stride is doubled, `<< 21` in place of `<< 20`. ECAM gives each bus 32
devices of 8 functions of 4 KiB, which is 20 bits, and a reader who counts the fields rather
than the bits can land on 21. Every device on bus 0 addresses correctly under this defect, which
is every device on both QEMU `virt` boards, so nothing in this tree's fixtures would notice.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert!(bdf.ecam_offset() + 0xfff < 0x1000_0000)`, at any bus
above 127, where a config read leaves the 256-bus window the kernel mapped and the volatile
accessor touches whatever is next in the address space.

diff --git a/crates/pci/src/lib.rs b/crates/pci/src/lib.rs
index a8fcf6f38..c42bc1f84 100644
--- a/crates/pci/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/pci/src/lib.rs
@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@ impl Bdf {
     /// `dev` and `func` are the 5- and 3-bit fields of the address; a larger value is masked to its
     /// field here, so it can never land on the next bus's or the next device's page.
     pub fn ecam_offset(self) -> u64 {
-        ((self.bus as u64) << 20)
+        ((self.bus as u64) << 21)
             | (((self.dev & 0x1f) as u64) << 15)
             | (((self.func & 0x7) as u64) << 12)
     }
